{"name":"Leviathan Purple","hex":"#8b2a98","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"leviathan","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A vast sea monster of tremendous strength, either imaginary or real, described as the most dangerous and powerful creature in the ocean.","A thing which is monstrously great in size, strength, etc. (especially a ship); also, a person with great power or wealth."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Very large; enormous, gargantuan."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/leviathan"},{"term":"purple","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A colour between red and blue; violet, though often closer to magenta.","Any non-spectral colour on the line of purples on a colour chromaticity diagram or a colour wheel between violet and red."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a purple hue.","Not predominantly red or blue, but having a mixture of Democrat and Republican support."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/purple"}],"entries":[{"title":"Leviathan","blocks":[{"text":"Leviathan is a giant sea serpent noted in theology and mythology. It is referenced in the Hebrew Bible, as a metaphor for a powerful enemy, notably Babylon. It is referred to in Psalms, the Book of Job, the Book of Isaiah, and the Book of Enoch. Leviathan is often an embodiment of chaos, threatening to eat the damned when their lives are over. In the end, it is annihilated. Christian theologians identified Leviathan with the demon of the deadly sin envy. According to Ophite Diagrams, Leviathan encapsulates the space of the material world."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9d/Destruction_of_Leviathan.png/330px-Destruction_of_Leviathan.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leviathan","wikidata":"Q192677"},{"title":"Purple","blocks":[{"text":"Purple is a color similar in appearance to violet light. In the RYB color model historically used in the arts, purple is a secondary color created by combining red and blue pigments. In the CMYK color model used in modern printing, purple is made by combining magenta pigment with cyan pigment, black pigment, or both. In the RGB color model used in computer and television screens, purple is created by mixing red and blue light in order to create colors that appear similar to violet light. According to color theory, purple is considered a cool color."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/Iris_sanguinea_cultivar%2C_Wakehurst_Place%2C_UK_-_Diliff.jpg/330px-Iris_sanguinea_cultivar%2C_Wakehurst_Place%2C_UK_-_Diliff.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Purple","wikidata":"Q3257809"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0b/Babylon%27s_world_view.jpg/500px-Babylon%27s_world_view.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0b/Babylon%27s_world_view.jpg/1280px-Babylon%27s_world_view.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0b/Babylon%27s_world_view.jpg/500px-Babylon%27s_world_view.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The circular salt sea Tiamat (green) and sphere of cosmic freshwater ocean Abzu are pre-existent deities in Sumerian myths, from whose mating Earth was created, so the sketch's side view shows the same as Babylon's map. Referring to the Atra-Hasis epic, Abzu is the celestial reservoir from which an organisation of younger gods triggers the Flood in order to consume humanity – also a source for the dangerous cosmic sea monster Leviathan.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABabylon's_world_view.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Lev-Beh-Ziz.jpg/500px-Lev-Beh-Ziz.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Lev-Beh-Ziz.jpg/500px-Lev-Beh-Ziz.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Leviathan the sea-monster, with Behemoth the land-monster and Ziz the air-monster. \"And on that day were two monsters parted, a female monster named Leviathan, to dwell in the abysses of the ocean over the fountains of the waters. But the male is named Behemoth, who occupied with his breast a waste wilderness named Duidain.\" (1 Enoch 60:7–8)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALev-Beh-Ziz.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/%D7%9E%D7%99%D7%9B%D7%90%D7%9C_%D7%A1%D7%92%D7%9F-%D7%9B%D7%94%D7%9F%2C_%D7%9C%D7%95%D7%99%D7%AA%D7%9F%2C_1983.jpg/500px-%D7%9E%D7%99%D7%9B%D7%90%D7%9C_%D7%A1%D7%92%D7%9F-%D7%9B%D7%94%D7%9F%2C_%D7%9C%D7%95%D7%99%D7%AA%D7%9F%2C_1983.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/%D7%9E%D7%99%D7%9B%D7%90%D7%9C_%D7%A1%D7%92%D7%9F-%D7%9B%D7%94%D7%9F%2C_%D7%9C%D7%95%D7%99%D7%AA%D7%9F%2C_1983.jpg/1280px-%D7%9E%D7%99%D7%9B%D7%90%D7%9C_%D7%A1%D7%92%D7%9F-%D7%9B%D7%94%D7%9F%2C_%D7%9C%D7%95%D7%99%D7%AA%D7%9F%2C_1983.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/%D7%9E%D7%99%D7%9B%D7%90%D7%9C_%D7%A1%D7%92%D7%9F-%D7%9B%D7%94%D7%9F%2C_%D7%9C%D7%95%D7%99%D7%AA%D7%9F%2C_1983.jpg/500px-%D7%9E%D7%99%D7%9B%D7%90%D7%9C_%D7%A1%D7%92%D7%9F-%D7%9B%D7%94%D7%9F%2C_%D7%9C%D7%95%D7%99%D7%AA%D7%9F%2C_1983.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Leviathan (1983), a painting by Michael Sgan-Cohen, the Israel Museum Collection, Jerusalem","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A%D7%9E%D7%99%D7%9B%D7%90%D7%9C_